  "notes": "Bluegrass-literate radio country — a rock/pop board with real acoustic-string playing underneath. Drums are the stated three-way split, and the radio setting is what this model encodes: \"the radio cuts use a modern layered kit (programmed clap on the backbeat, 16th shaker, sampled kick under the acoustic kick), the acoustic cuts drop to brushes and a two-beat, and the fastest cuts sit on a train beat.\" So the snare carries a clap layer, the shaker runs 16ths and the hat sits between the two subdivisions. Harmony follows the entry: \"I–IV–V and I–V–vi–IV, with I–♭VII–IV on the rockers and mixolydian vamps on the acoustic cuts.\" The most useful melodic claim is a compositional order rather than a shape: \"his co-producer's piano-first background shows up as chord-forward writing (choruses harmonised before they are melodised),\" which is why `chordToneBias` sits high while the line itself is \"mid-tenor, rhythmically busy, with a wide chorus leap.\" ⚠ The programmed sub on the radio cuts is described as reinforcement under a live bass, not as the low end, so no `drums.bass808` block is authored and the model keeps the genre's inherited live bass; the entry's upright on the bluegrass material is the low register floor instead. ⚠ Boundary flag: the fastest cuts (a 182 reading folding to ~91) are train-beat material and belong to `country-train`. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: medium-high); the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the perc density, the clap offset and the counter ratio.",
